{
  "dom_id": "step-q143304",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{36}∼N(0,{18}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $36$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ | $\\,0.6103\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | $\\,0.6480\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{{18}^{2}}{36}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8) & =1-P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<0.8) \\\\  & =1-P(-0.8<\\overset{―}{X}<0.8) \\\\  & =1-P(\\frac{-0.8-0}{\\sqrt{9}}<Z<\\frac{0.8-0}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =1-P(-0.27<Z<0.27) \\\\  & =1-(Φ(0.27)-Φ(-0.27)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-0.27)-Φ(0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.27) & =0.6064,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.27) & =1-Φ(0.27) \\\\  & =1-0.6064 \\\\  & =0.3936.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8) & =1+Φ(-0.27)-Φ(0.27) \\\\  & =1+0.3936-0.6064 \\\\  & =0.7872.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
